arm64: dts: yupik: attach the WCN6750 Bluetooth to the HS UART as a serdev

The vendor tree describes Bluetooth as a bare soc-level node,
soc/bt_wcn6750, carrying ONLY power rails and GPIOs and no transport at
all. That suits Qualcomm's downstream design, where a proprietary Android
userspace opens the tty directly and btpower.ko just sequences the rails.
There is no such userspace on postmarketOS, so nothing ever creates an HCI
device and Settings shows "No Bluetooth found".

The mainline model instead expects the BT chip to be a CHILD of the UART it
hangs off, so the serdev core instantiates it and hci_uart/hci_qca drives
it. This adds that child.

Everything here is copied from the board's own bt_wcn6750 node so the
hardware description stays identical, only re-expressed in the bindings the
in-tree driver understands:

    enable-gpios   <&tlmm 85 0>   was qcom,bt-reset-gpio  (BT_EN)
    swctrl-gpios   <&tlmm 86 0>   was qcom,bt-sw-ctrl-gpio
    vddio          &L19B          was qcom,bt-vdd-io-supply
    vddaon         &S7B           was qcom,bt-vdd-aon-supply
    vddbtcxmx      &S7B           was qcom,bt-vdd-dig-supply
    vddrfacmn      &S7B           was qcom,bt-vdd-rfacmn-supply
    vddrfa0p8      &S7B           was qcom,bt-vdd-rfa-0p8-supply
    vddrfa1p7      &S1B           was qcom,bt-vdd-rfa1-supply
    vddrfa1p2      &S8B           was qcom,bt-vdd-rfa2-supply
    vddasd         &L11C          was qcom,bt-vdd-asd-supply

qupv3_se7_4uart is the right UART: yupik.dtsi aliases it as hsuart0, and it
is the only high-speed UART enabled on this board.

The old soc-level node is deliberately LEFT ENABLED. Disabling it was the
first attempt and it wedged the boot: btpower.ko and bt_fm_slim.ko both
load at boot for audio, and pulling their node out from under them stalled
the rootfs before networking came up (gadget enumerated, carrier 0, no
PMOS_LOGS export, so the initramfs had already handed over). Measured
2026-08-03.

Two drivers touching one radio's rails is still a concern, but it is a
manageable one: hci_qca is built as a MODULE and blacklisted, so nothing
binds this node until it is loaded deliberately. That also makes the
failure debuggable over SSH instead of by flash-and-pray.

firmware-name selects the NVM file; btqca.c prefixes "qca/", so this
resolves to qca/msnv11.bin. The rampatch name is derived from the ROM
version by the driver (qca/msbtfw%02x.mbn) - see bt-wcn6750-support.patch.
Both files ship in the vendor firmware package already installed on this
device.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/vendor/qcom/yupik.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/vendor/qcom/yupik.dtsi
@@ -5498,4 +5498,26 @@
-&qupv3_se7_4uart {
-	status = "ok";
-};
+&qupv3_se7_4uart {
+	status = "ok";
+
+	/* WCN6750 Bluetooth, as a serdev child so hci_qca can bind. */
+	bt_serdev: bluetooth {
+		compatible = "qcom,wcn6750-bt";
+		pinctrl-names = "default";
+		pinctrl-0 = <&bt_en_sleep>;
+
+		enable-gpios = <&tlmm 85 0>;
+		swctrl-gpios = <&tlmm 86 0>;
+
+		vddio-supply = <&L19B>;
+		vddaon-supply = <&S7B>;
+		vddbtcxmx-supply = <&S7B>;
+		vddrfacmn-supply = <&S7B>;
+		vddrfa0p8-supply = <&S7B>;
+		vddrfa1p7-supply = <&S1B>;
+		vddrfa1p2-supply = <&S8B>;
+		vddasd-supply = <&L11C>;
+
+		max-speed = <3200000>;
+		firmware-name = "msnv11.bin";
+	};
+};

 
